{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01361oCkeV5c7K9pokVeGjSG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Wrote **`03-005-how-define-and-enforce-agent-autonomy-boundaries.md`** covering:\n\n- **Concept:** Boundaries are encoded in the system (orchestrator + tool registry), not just in the system prompt — the LLM can reason around prose instructions but can't override a `PolicyViolation` exception.\n- **Mechanism:** Six concrete layers — capability scoping (tool allowlists), runtime policy middleware (pre-call checks), resource/cost/turn budgets, HITL triggers on irreversible actions (`LangGraph interrupt_before`), sandboxed execution (E2B/Modal), and audit trail (LangSmith/OpenTelemetry).\n- **Tradeoff:** Too-tight boundaries → constant escalation and user frustration; too-loose → one prompt injection causes real harm. Calibration strategy: start read-only, add write tools one at a time with HITL, remove HITL only after stable production observation.\n- **Key pitfall called out:** Putting constraints only in the system prompt — the canonical weak-candidate mistake.\n\nNext item in the queue is **Q6: Essential components of an agent beyond an LLM?**"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":349,"cache_read_input_tokens":61513,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":349,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c677d794-f042-48fb-8fc1-ed97073d9370","uuid":"5029f067-db47-49a2-b409-15228629e730"}
